PnL optimisation: code changes after the SPEC froze (SPEC 'walk_forward' DISCIPLINE). Every dev run is in
results/runs.jsonl. Full reruns after the first full results: at most 2, only for documented bugs.

BEFORE ANY DEV RESULT
- sim.py: zero-size residue orders after full exits (float residue in the inc/red split) removed with a relative
  tolerance; absolute size thresholds replaced by relative ones; equity renormalised to 1 at each UTC day start
  (exact: every PnL term is linear in size). Found when ruinous rules (E < 1e-12) broke the size thresholds.
- sim.py: per-decision maker window (policy.K_at) so a model can switch cadence at a fold boundary.
- Spec arithmetic: Track 1 uses 42 intraday columns, not 41 (48 F-CORE minus 6 dropped).
- sigma60 is floored at 0.5 bps per minute like sigma_t (prep.py), for flat-price minutes.
- Plain fade on the 1-min grid uses the calibration-study sigma (EWMA lambda 0.94, i.e. span 32.3 minutes).
- 60-min fade: |z60| ranked among the trailing 30 days of 1-min values (43,200), decisions every minute.
- Ridge CV purges with the largest H of the grid for every H (slightly more purge than needed); the final fit uses
  the same Hmax-purged training rows.
- Net inner validation reuses the Track 1 cells for T2-NET with their own (Track 1) inner-validation windows, which
  differ from the Track 2 windows by at most a few days.
- Net feature-group dropout zeroes a group for a whole segment (not per row).

AFTER THE FIRST RIDGE DEV RUN (T1-LIN, T2-LIN only; no number changed)
- sim.py returns per-day fees and funding; run_dev.py stores them; gate.py passes engine-E fees to p_sel
  (daily_cost) so the skill test's cost is exact rather than charging exposure drift as trading.
- checks.py: three checks were mis-specified and were corrected before they could gate anything:
  funding alignment now uses realistic fills (nominal fills at the decision close pay the next print by
  construction, as engine F does); the fold manifest checks the purge the code actually applies; the shuffled-label
  check trades sign(mu) every minute so that it is not trivially zero.

DECLARED BEFORE ANY NET DEV-OOS RESULT WAS COMPUTED (2026-11-01, after the inner-validation logs only)
- Finding: every Track 1 MLP cell (and some Track 2 h = 60 MLP seeds) never left the flat start. After the
  2-epoch band-off warm-up (2-10 gradient steps on Track 1) |tau| stays inside the initial +-0.05 band around
  p = 0; a hard band passes no gradient while the position never moves, so training freezes at cash. The SPEC
  fallback (band collapse with b > 0.9) does not fire on this pattern. The main procedure is NOT changed.
- Descriptive variant, not gate-eligible, not deployable: T1-NET-W and T2-NET-W. MLP cells are retrained with the
  band-off warm-up extended to at least 60 gradient steps (otherwise identical; LIN cells unchanged), then the
  same in-fold cell choice over all cells. Reported next to T1-NET/T2-NET; its 12 cells per track are added to
  the Deflated-Sharpe N.
- Variant implementation note: the first variant launch stopped early because patience counted from epoch 0
  (U = 0 ties during the band-off warm-up). For the variant only, patience now starts when the band switches on;
  the SPEC procedure's stopping rule is unchanged. A test run (T1 Y2021 h = 1, seed 0) still stayed at cash after
  78 band-off steps: with costs in the band-off loss, the MLP learns tau close to 0.

PRE-RESULTS CODE REVIEW (independent reviewers plus adversarial verifiers; before the first full dev run)
Only T1-LIN and T2-LIN had been scored (rows in runs.jsonl dated before this entry); they are superseded and rerun.
- Exposure alignment (gate G1 / p_sel, check 12): engine E records x at the close of bar t BEFORE the decision at t,
  so the recorded path is one bar behind the position that earns each bar. gate.py now passes the start-of-bar
  exposure of each bar's own position (lead(held(x))) and funding on the previous bar's position. A test shows the
  engine-F daily PnL of that path matches engine E to about 2 bps RMS per day (intraday compounding), 13x better
  than before.
- BandPolicy rounded the band edge OUTWARD to the 0.05 grid, so x stayed outside its own band and was rebalanced
  every decision (one fill per minute in a test). Rounding is now toward the band interior (policy.band_target and
  fast.band_chain).
- Drift trim used q, not q plus committed taker orders: under S it could over-reduce into a stray opposite position
  and strand EventPolicy in 'entry'. The trim now uses the effective exposure; EventPolicy exits a stray position
  on a cancel.
- A new decision now also cancels a chase that is queued but not yet executed (it was filling after the policy had
  exited, costing two taker fees and desynchronising HysteresisPolicy). A chased order replaced in the same
  direction keeps its price and clock, so decisions faster than K cannot postpone the chase.
- The SPEC's '|x| > 1.02 at a decision' trim was missing; added (taker, logged as a trim).
- B5 static-exposure-matched held constant contracts; it now holds constant exposure (rebalanced at 23:59 when
  |x - mean x| > 0.02; rules.StaticPolicy).
- EventPolicy under nominal fills held H-1 bars; fixed to H.
- Trade log: gross is now realised PnL / entry equity (exposure-weighted; price_move_bps keeps the full-size move);
  a one-fill flip's fee is split pro rata.
- Check 12 now fails if a required strategy is missing (it passed vacuously before).
- Net scaler is fitted on training rows only [a, tr_end) (it included the inner-validation rows); all nets and the
  NET-W variant are retrained from scratch before any net dev-OOS result.
- New regression tests for each item (56 tests pass).
- From the statistics/seal reviewer: G1 now uses each candidate's own single-step max-t adjusted p (the track
  p_sel is the best candidate's); p_sel's daily cost is engine-F gross minus engine E's daily return, so the observed
  statistic is exactly engine E's and every shift keeps the strategy's own execution cost (fees, stale-limit maker
  fills, latency, intraday compounding); check 12 compounds the per-bar path within each day (a looser bound for the
  plain fade, whose late maker fills the path cannot see); the seal guard hashes the files actually loaded (by role),
  uses a fixed lock path, ties the run to the pre-registered code commit, preflights T1-NT inference before the lock,
  and data.load(seal=True) now also needs the committed prereg.json and the lock; prereg.py refuses a dirty tree.
- Deflated Sharpe (reported, not a gate): the trial-Sharpe variance is now 1/T (the null sampling variance of a daily
  Sharpe). The first version took the variance across the track's candidates and comparators, which mixes very
  different strategies (e.g. the ruinous intraday trend) and drove every DSR to 0.
- Secondary information-only seal hypothesis S1 declared before pre-registration (prereg.json): T1-LIN at C10-S.

AFTER THE FIRST FULL DEV RESULTS (documented bug; full rerun 1 of at most 2)
- Bug: under C4 an exit rests as a maker order; the cap trims (1.02 at a decision, 1.10 at a close) ignored it, so
  a short that drifted past 1.02 while its buy-back rested was trimmed AND then fully bought back by the chase,
  leaving a small stray long (0.02-0.04x) that merged later trades (FADEV C4-B showed 425 'trades' instead of
  about 5,900). Fix: trims use the committed exposure including a resting reduce/flip order; EventPolicy flattens
  any stray position before a new entry. Only C4 rows (C4-B, C4-S) can change; C7 exits are taker orders already
  counted. Regression test reproduces the old failure. All strategies are rerun once with the fixed code, together
  with T1-NT; rows of the first full run in runs.jsonl are superseded.
- Descriptive fee-tier sensitivity (diag_fees.py) is rerun after the fix.
- Second focused review (before the rerun), four more confirmed defects, fixed with regression tests (61 pass):
  (a) trims now act on the effective exposure and shrink a resting order's reduce leg by the trimmed amount (the
      first fix counted a resting C4 FLIP as committed and could trim the wrong way); C4 rows only;
  (b) HysteresisPolicy ignores a stale fill whose sign disagrees with its decided state (under C10-S with H = 1 and
      every-bar decisions a stale entry fill re-set the state and blocked later entries); C10-S rows of T1-LIN;
  (c) an order re-issued in the same direction and of the same kind keeps its price and clock whether or not it
      chases (under S a target repeated every bar cancelled its maker order before its window); latent: no current
      selection decides every bar with a resting maker order;
  (d) the 1.02 decision trim fires only at a policy's decision bars (SignChangePolicy gets its decision mask; B&H's
      only decision is the first); intraday trend and B&H rows change slightly.

DOCUMENTATION NOTE (added after the seal; no code or result changed)
- checks.py runs the causality check on 20 random cuts of one 150k-bar window (noise and permutation), not the
  1,000 cuts the SPEC names. This reduction was not logged before; it is recorded here. Feature causality was
  also confirmed at the seal: the features recomputed on the full data were bit-identical on every dev row.

VIEWER (descriptive, after the seal; no result changed)
- sim.py records per-bar log equity and the entry/exit prices of each trade (recording only; 61 tests pass).
- viz_export.py re-simulates the 9 shown strategies at C7-B for dev OOS and for the sealed year with the frozen
  fold-S models, asserts that every daily PnL series is bit-identical to the scored results (dev_daily, seal_daily),
  and writes the viewer data. This re-read of the sealed bars is logged in results/seal_access.log; it reproduces
  the scored numbers and re-scores nothing.
- The viewer export ran a second time after an independent data and interaction check (a second logged seal re-read;
  bit-identity asserted again). Fixes: the last PnL point of each run includes the final close-out fee and the sealed
  run is chained from the scored dev total; holds are wall-clock minutes (the seal's missing day); every fill is a
  position point; the page gives Plotly UTC date strings (it read numeric times in the viewer's local time zone),
  aligns all panels on the price grid for the shared hover, drops stale loads, and labels the dev return as log.
  Monthly 1-minute prices ship as base64 text (artifacts do not serve raw binary).
- Phone version (viz_template.html and viz_tests/ only; the data files and viz_export.py are unchanged; no seal
  re-read). The page draws only the view (plus one screen each side, min/max per pixel) and reads the cursor values
  from full-resolution data. Measured in Chromium phone emulation with the CPU slowed 4x: first draw about 20-25 s
  -> about 3.3 s, strategy switch 5-15 s -> about 0.4 s, 1-minute zoom 18-30 s -> about 0.9 s; on a 390 px phone the
  chart starts at about 500 px from the top instead of about 1000 px. Touch: a horizontal swipe pans, a pinch zooms,
  a tap pins the readout, a vertical swipe scrolls the page; zoom buttons, one range row, a strategy dropdown.
- Four independent review rounds checked the phone version and the desktop mouse path. Each finding was reproduced
  by a separate verifier before it was fixed, and each round re-tested the fixes of the round before. Fixed: double
  and triple clicks (no stray pins, also on a slow CPU), gestures across finger-count changes, the exact 1-minute
  readout price with an hourly fallback for failed or stalled loads, landscape phones (readout, chart and zoom bar on
  one screen), compact axes with at least 2 distinct labels, short charts, height-only resizes, a constant readout
  height. viz_tests/ holds one regression check per confirmed finding (README). Limits: emulated phones in Chromium
  only (no real devices, no Safari/WebKit); headless Chromium draws system-ui in the wider Inter font, so text wraps
  were judged with Roboto.
- Real-phone fix (viewer only): on an Android phone the price panel showed a fan of grey lines from its bottom-left
  corner to the trade marks. The hold lines (entry -> exit) were a WebGL line with a null between trades; the GPU
  gets NaN there and many phone GPUs draw NaN at the corner. Headless Chromium renders WebGL in software, so the
  emulated tests did not show it. The hold lines are now one SVG path shape; a hold shorter than 4 px (hidden under
  its own entry and exit marks) is left out. No WebGL trace carries a null (viz_tests/regress_gl_gaps.js). On a 4x
  slower CPU the busiest strategy (T1-FADEV, 7015 trades) switches in about 0.46 s instead of 0.8-0.9 s in the
  "All" view.
- The viewer opens on T2-P (the Track-2 information-only candidate, sealed verdict CONSISTENT) instead of T1-LIN
  (KILL); viz_build.py embeds its file. It remembers a strategy only when the user picks one (the old key also stored
  the default). results/outlook.md: the best result and its outlook, descriptive, from the scored files and an
  independent check; nothing re-scored or re-chosen. The plain trend rule tsmom_unscaled did slightly better than T2-P
  in every period; the evidence for an edge stays weak (not CONFIRMED; pre-registered decision CASH).
- 2026-11-03, new-data test (prereg_newdata.md, nd_fetch.py, nd_run.py): T2-P, frozen, on markets and periods the
  study never scored: 80 Binance USD-M USDT perps listed by 2020-12 (delisted ones included), 2021-01..2026-10, and BTC
  2017-2019 (spot), 2020 and 2026-10-31..11-02. Engine H (hourly) reproduces engine E on BTC (dev gap <= 0.06 %/yr).
  Before scoring, two independent review rounds (causality, engine accounting with an independent reference engine,
  scoring and pre-registration consistency; then a fix verification) led to: archive days filled from the daily
  archives, the 1.10 trim before the rule decides, funding inside data holes paid by the position held across the
  hole, ruin handling, per-market statistics from simple returns, a stronger run-once guard (clean tree, SHA-256 data
  manifest, committed lock copy, one change-log entry per re-run), and the liquid-20 list fixed in the
  pre-registration. The BTC validation did not change.
